      },
      "extractionMethod": "llm_extraction_huggingface"
    },
    {
      "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#dimension_fd91c8bd",
      "@type": "AnalogicalMapping",
      "instance_name": "mapping_id_1",
      "label": "dog is a 'free blanket' recipient",
      "description": "The text 'free blanket' is mapped onto the visual entity 'dog_wearing_headdress'. The meme implies that the dog (representing a Native American) is receiving a 'free blanket' (a symbol of colonial 'gifts' or coercion), creating an analogical mapping where the dog is portrayed as the recipient of this symbolic offering. This mapping is part of the racist trope that portrays Native Americans as grateful for colonial oppression.",
      "belongsToAnalysis": {
        "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#analysis_6c45a031"
      },
      "extractionMethod": "llm_extraction_huggingface"
    },
    {
      "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#dimension_3b8edc9e",
      "@type": "SemioticProjection",
      "instance_name": "semiotic_projection_1",
      "label": "User projected onto the dog wearing a Native American headdress",
      "description": "The user is projected onto the visual material of the dog wearing the headdress, which serves as a stand-in for a Native American person in the meme's racist trope. The user identifies with or imagines themselves as the recipient of the 'free blanket' (a symbol of colonial coercion), thereby projecting their own perspective onto the dog's role in the meme's narrative. This projection is implicit, as there are no explicit deictic pronouns like 'me' or 'you', but the meme's structure invites the user to identify with the dog as the subject of the colonial 'gift' and the 'grateful' recipient.",
      "belongsToAnalysis": {
        "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#analysis_6c45a031"
      },
      "extractionMethod": "llm_extraction_huggingface"
    },
    {
      "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#dimension_1bb8a667",
      "@type": "ToxicityAssessment",
      "instance_name": "toxicity_1",
      "label": "Harmful",
      "description": "The meme depicts a dog wearing a Native American headdress, juxtaposed with the text 'free blanket' and 'maybe white man not so bad after all.' This visual and textual combination is a racist trope that appropriates and mocks Native American culture. The headdress is a sacred and culturally significant item, and its use in this context trivializes it. The meme's intent is to mock Native Americans by implying they are somehow 'grateful' or 'accepting' of white colonialism, which is a harmful and offensive stereotype. It perpetuates the idea that Native Americans are inferior or subservient, and it reinforces historical narratives of exploitation and cultural erasure. The meme is harmful because it normalizes and spreads racist imagery and language, potentially causing emotional distress to Native American individuals and communities, and it contributes to the ongoing marginalization and stereotyping of Indigenous peoples.",
      "belongsToAnalysis": {
        "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#analysis_6c45a031"
      },
      "extractionMethod": "llm_extraction_huggingface"
    },
    {
      "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#dimension_cc59079c",
      "@type": "TargetCommunity",
      "instance_name": "audience_id",
      "label": "audience description",
      "description": "The target audience is individuals who are familiar with or find humor in racist stereotypes and historical appropriation, particularly those who may not recognize the offensive nature of the Native American headdress and the 'free blanket' trope. The meme is designed to appeal to a community that finds amusement in mocking Indigenous peoples by implying their 'gratitude' for colonialism, often through the lens of cultural appropriation and historical ignorance. This audience may include people who engage with internet humor that relies on offensive tropes, and who may not be aware of the harm caused by such imagery.",
      "belongsToAnalysis": {
        "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#analysis_6c45a031"
      },
      "extractionMethod": "llm_extraction_huggingface"
